11520,9407,220,22,15,24884,43298,284,1124,15940,220,17,15,24884,43298,57758,323,1124,11520,9407,220,20,15,24884,43298,284,1124,15940,220,19,15,24884,43298,59,568,2055,11,582,646,18130,279,7493,438,510,59,9640,59,9407,220,16,15,24884,43298,1124,9407,220,18,15,24884,43298,1124,15940,220,19,15,24884,43298,1124,15940,220,17,15,24884,43298,198,59,2533,5847,11,582,990,279,1985,4686,75811,39421,323,279,9569,369,1124,11520,9407,220,18,15,24884,43298,284,1124,37018,35702,26888,90,18,3417,90,17,11035,982,59,9640,59,9407,220,16,15,24884,43298,1124,50853,1124,37018,35702,26888,90,18,3417,90,17,92,1124,50853,1124,15940,220,19,15,24884,43298,1124,50853,1124,15940,220,17,15,24884,43298,284,1124,37018,35702,26888,90,18,3417,90,17,92,1124,9407,220,16,15,24884,43298,1124,15940,220,19,15,24884,43298,1124,15940,220,17,15,24884,43298,198,59,2533],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nSimplify $\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"95e63194ea76ee066ae6423fd0f190c796043c4abaa79eac83d8021d6da4be91"},"seconds":47.178903579711914,"seed":186021987540501677,"stop":"eos","text":"We will now use the product-to-sum identities for the product of sines and cosines. Recall that:\n\\[\n\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (A - B) - \\cos (A + B)]\n\\]\nApplying this to \\(\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (40^\\circ - 20^\\circ) - \\cos (40^\\circ + 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 20^\\circ - \\cos 60^\\circ]\n\\]\nSince \\(\\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this back into our expression, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\right)\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the product-to-sum identity for \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (10^\\circ - 20^\\circ) + \\cos (10^\\circ + 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (-10^\\circ) + \\cos 30^\\circ] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 10^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this back, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\right) - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ + \\frac{3}{16} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{3}{16}\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified value of the given expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To simplify the expression \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ\\), we will use trigonometric identities and properties of complementary angles. Let's proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, recall that \\(\\cos 70^\\circ = \\sin 20^\\circ\\) and \\(\\cos 50^\\circ = \\sin 40^\\circ\\). So, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the product-to-sum identities and the identity for \\(\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\):\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\sin 40^\\circ \\cdot \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe will now use the product-to-sum identities for the product of sines and cosines. Recall that:\n\\[\n\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (A - B) - \\cos (A + B)]\n\\]\nApplying this to \\(\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (40^\\circ - 20^\\circ) - \\cos (40^\\circ + 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 20^\\circ - \\cos 60^\\circ]\n\\]\nSince \\(\\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this back into our expression, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\right)\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the product-to-sum identity for \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (10^\\circ - 20^\\circ) + \\cos (10^\\circ + 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (-10^\\circ) + \\cos 30^\\circ] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 10^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this back, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\right) - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ + \\frac{3}{16} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{3}{16}\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified value of the given expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}\n\\]","id":"precalculus:405"}
